# What do firms really maximise?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Profit, size, or the managers' comfort? Friedman's 1970 essay and the stakeholder reply"
next: {"title":"Why pay a chief executive so much?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/115-why-pay-a-chief-executive-so-much.cast"}

## Who steers the boatyard?
domain: {"x":[0,72],"y":[0,2950]}
sources:
    - id: berle
      title: The Modern Corporation and Private Property
      authors: Adolf A. Berle and Gardiner C. Means
      year: 1932
      finding: Of the 200 largest US non-financial corporations (data around 1929–30), 88 (44%), holding 58% of the group's assets, had no owner with even 20% of the stock and were classed as management-controlled (as summarised in Mizruchi 2004, 'Berle and Means revisited', Theory and Society 33).
    - id: baumol
      title: Business Behavior, Value and Growth
      authors: William J. Baumol
      year: 1959
      finding: Managers of large firms maximise sales revenue subject to a minimum profit constraint acceptable to shareholders; output is larger and price lower than under profit maximisation.

    text tag_a "a: profit" #2f6b8f x 540 y 622 font_size 38 hidden true
    text tag_b "b: something else" #b5482e x 800 y 622 font_size 38 hidden true
    text fact_t "Berle & Means, 1932: 200 largest US firms" #3d3833 cites ["berle"] x 500 y 540 font_size 44 hidden true
    text fact_44 "44%" #b5482e x 320 y 420 font_size 130 hidden true
    text fact_44s "of firms" #3d3833 x 320 y 320 font_size 40 hidden true
    text fact_58 "58%" #b5482e x 680 y 420 font_size 130 hidden true
    text fact_58s "of assets" #3d3833 x 680 y 320 font_size 40 hidden true
    text fact_cue "run by hired managers" #b5482e x 500 y 215 font_size 48 hidden true
    axes axes x_label "Boats a year (Q)" y_label "$ thousands a year" cross corner ticks.x [10,20,30,40,50] ticks.y [500,1000,1500,2000,2500] hidden true
    icon yard at.data [64,1650] size 70 set twemoji icon_look picture of sailboat hidden true
    text yard_l "the boatyard" #8f887c at.data [64,1380] font_size 26 hidden true
    curve R #2f6b8f expr x*(100-x) x_from 0 x_to 70 hidden true
    label label_R "Revenue" right #2f6b8f attach_to R hidden true
    curve pi #b5482e expr "70*x - x*x - 400" x_from 6.4 x_to 63.6 hidden true
    label label_pi "Profit" right #b5482e attach_to pi hidden true
    dot A #b5482e at.on pi at.x 35 hidden true
    text A_read "$825k" #b5482e at.data [35,1040] font_size 30 hidden true
    curve floor #8f887c expr "725 + 0*x" x_from 0 x_to 70 dash true hidden true
    text floor_l "owners' floor $725k" #8f887c at.data [60,820] font_size 26 hidden true
    dot c35 #3d3833 at.on pi at.x 35 hidden true
    dot c45 #3d3833 at.on pi at.x 45 hidden true
    dot c50 #3d3833 at.on pi at.x 50 hidden true
    text c35_l "35" #3d3833 at.data [35,1230] font_size 30 hidden true
    text c45_l "45" #3d3833 at.data [45,1000] font_size 30 hidden true
    text c50_l "50" #3d3833 at.data [47,450] font_size 30 hidden true
    group o35 members ["c35","c35_l"] hidden true
    group o45 members ["c45","c45_l"] hidden true
    group o50 members ["c50","c50_l"] hidden true
    dot B #2f6b8f at.on pi at.x 45 hidden true
    curve B_guide #8f887c x_expr "45" y_expr t t_from 0 t_to 725 dash true hidden true
    text B_read "45 boats, $725k" #2f6b8f at.data [56,1060] font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "Who steers the boatyard?"

What do firms really maximise? The textbook says pro (@draw tag_a@)fit, and I'm going to defend that.

B: And I'll argue (@draw tag_b@) for something else: size, or the managers' own comfort.

    pause 0.3

B: Exhibit one. In 1932 Adolf Berle and Gardiner Means studied Ame (@draw fact_t@)rica's two hundred largest companies, using figures from 1929.

B: In for (@draw fact_44 fact_44s@)ty-four percent of them, holding fifty-eight  (@draw fact_58 fact_58s@)percent of the assets, no owner had even a fifth of the shares.

B: Hired managers ran them.
    draw fact_cue

Fair point. Owners who each hold a sliver can't watch closely. So who decides what the firm aims at?
    highlight fact_cue effect underline

    erase fact_t fact_44 fact_44s fact_58 fact_58s fact_cue

So take Ha (@draw axes yard yard_l@)rbourtown's boatyard: owned by, say, a thousand townspeople, and run by a hired manager.

B: Its sales revenue, in  (@draw R label_R@)illustrative numbers: it climbs with each boat, then flattens at fifty boats, because selling more means cutting the price.

Profit  (@draw pi label_pi@)is revenue minus cost. It peaks earlier: thirty-five boats,

and eight hundred and twenty-five thousand dollars.
    draw A A_read

B: Now William Baumol's idea, from 1959: a manager's pay, staff and standing grow (@highlight R label_R@) with sales, not profit. So she wants sales, as long as the owners stay quiet.

Say the owners stay quiet w (@draw floor floor_l@)hile profit is at least seven hundred and twenty-five thousand.

    draw o35 o45 o50 parallel true

    hide tag_a tag_b

    ask "Where does the sales-minded manager stop? Tap a point." choose ["o35","o45","o50"] answer o45 stage own voice b
        intro: Your guess: how many boats does the sales-minded manager build?
        right: Forty-five, yes.
        wrong: She sells past the peak until profit hits the floor: here, forty-five.

    erase o35 o50 o45

    show tag_a tag_b

Forty- (@draw B B_guide B_read@)five: as far down the profit curve as the floor allows.

Ten boats more than the owners' best, and a hundred thousand dollars less for them.
    fade A A_read to 0.35

    pause 0.4

## Empire or quiet life?
domain: {"x":[0,72],"y":[0,2950]}
vars:
    f:
      value: 725
      min: 400
      max: 825
      step: 5
      label: Owners' floor ($k)
    qb:
      expr: min(50, 35 + sqrt(max(0, 825 - f)))
sources:
    - id: simon
      title: A Behavioral Model of Rational Choice
      authors: Herbert A. Simon
      year: 1955
      finding: 'Decision makers with limited information and computing capacity search for alternatives that meet an aspiration level and stop, rather than optimise; Simon later named this ''satisficing'' (Psychological Review, 1956). Quarterly Journal of Economics 69(1): 99–118.'
    - id: manne
      title: Mergers and the Market for Corporate Control
      authors: Henry G. Manne
      year: 1965
      finding: 'A poorly managed firm''s low share price invites outsiders to buy control and replace management, disciplining managers. Journal of Political Economy 73(2): 110–120.'
    - id: bm
      title: Enjoying the Quiet Life? Corporate Governance and Managerial Preferences
      authors: Marianne Bertrand and Sendhil Mullainathan
      year: 2003
      finding: 'After US state antitakeover laws, wages in shielded firms rose (about 1% for production workers, about 4% for white-collar workers), both the destruction of old plants and the creation of new plants fell, and productivity and profitability declined: managers prefer a quiet life rather than empire building. Journal of Political Economy 111(5): 1043–1075.'

    text tag_a "a: profit" #2f6b8f x 540 y 622 font_size 38 hidden true
    text tag_b "b: something else" #b5482e x 800 y 622 font_size 38 hidden true
    axes axes x_label "Boats a year (Q)" y_label "$ thousands a year" cross corner ticks.x [10,20,30,40,50] ticks.y [500,1000,1500,2000,2500] hidden true
    curve R #2f6b8f expr x*(100-x) x_from 0 x_to 70 hidden true
    label label_R "Revenue" right #2f6b8f attach_to R hidden true
    curve pi #b5482e expr "70*x - x*x - 400" x_from 6.4 x_to 63.6 hidden true
    label label_pi "Profit" right #b5482e attach_to pi hidden true
    dot A #b5482e at.on pi at.x 35 style.opacity 0.45 hidden true
    curve floor #8f887c expr "f + 0*x" x_from 0 x_to 70 dash true hidden true
    region band x_from 25 x_to 45 between ["pi","floor"] style.fill #f2c14e hidden true
    text band_l "good enough" #3d3833 at.data [35,1050] font_size 32 hidden true
    dot B #2f6b8f at.on pi at.x 45 bind {"at.x":"qb"} hidden true
    curve B_guide #8f887c x_expr "qb + 0*t" y_expr t t_from 0 t_to 725 bind.t_to f dash true hidden true
    text readout "owners' floor ${f:0}k → {qb:0} boats" #2f6b8f at.data [42,1500] font_size 34 hidden true
    text simon "Herbert Simon: satisficing" #3d3833 at.data [20,2650] font_size 32 hidden true
    text bidder "takeover: the next bidder" #8a5fa8 at.data [20,2650] cites ["manne"] font_size 32 hidden true
    group plot members ["axes","R","label_R","pi","label_pi","A","floor","B","B_guide","readout"] hidden true
    scratch ev cites ["bm"] work ["US states make takeovers harder (1980s)","white-collar pay: +4%","fewer plants closed, fewer opened","productivity and profit: down"] x 500 y 380 font_size 40 hidden true
    text quiet "a quiet life, not an empire" #b5482e x 500 y 170 font_size 46 hidden true
    text conf1 "drift when unwatched: mostly agreed" #3d3833 x 500 y 360 font_size 46 hidden true
    text conf2 "toward size or comfort: contested" #b5482e x 500 y 270 font_size 46 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after"}] then after hidden true
    scratch m work [{"tex":"\\text{profit} = Q(100-Q) - (400 + 30Q) = 70Q - Q^2 - 400"},{"tex":"\\text{top: } 70 - 2Q = 0 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad Q = 35"},{"tex":"70Q - Q^2 - 400 = 725 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad Q = 25 \\text{ or } 45"}] x 500 y 380 font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "Empire or quiet life?"

    draw tag_a tag_b axes R label_R pi label_pi A floor B B_guide parallel true

So a manager with slack  (@draw readout@)can swap the owners' profit for size.

Try it: drag the owners' floor and watch her output move.
    explore params ["f"]

B: Raise the floor to the owners' best, eight hundred and twenty-five thousand, and her slack is gone: thirty-five boats.
    animate f 825 duration 2.5

    animate f 725 duration 1.5

B: But Her (@draw simon@)bert Simon doubted anyone maximises at all. Firms take the first plan that clears the bar and stop looking. He called it satisficing.

On this pic (@draw band band_l@)ture, anything from twenty-five to forty-five boats is good enough. The peak is just one of many.

    erase simon

    erase band band_l

Here's my reply. A boatyard run below it (@draw bidder@)s best is a bargain: a raider can buy the shares, sack the manager, and pocket the difference.

That's what Henry Manne called the market for corporate (@animate f 800 duration 2.5@) control, in 1965. The real floor is set by the next bidder, not by sleepy owners.

    erase bidder

B: Then take the bidder away and watch. In the 1980s, many US states passed laws that made hostile takeovers harder.
    fade plot to 0.12

    draw ev_box ev_line_1

B: Marianne Bertrand and Sendhil Mullainathan compared firms the laws shielded with firms they didn't.
    highlight ev_line_1 effect underline

In the shield (@draw ev_line_2@)ed firms, white-collar pay rose about four percent.

Fewe (@draw ev_line_3@)r old plants closed, fewer new ones opened, a (@draw ev_line_4@)nd productivity and profits fell.

B: So not an empire (@draw quiet@). A quiet life. Your bidder matters, but unwatched managers drift toward comfort, not size.

    erase ev quiet

I'll take that. Managers drift wh (@draw conf1@)en nobody can strike: mostly agreed. Toward size o (@draw conf2@)r toward comfort: contested.

    pause 0.4

    erase conf1 conf2

    fade plot to 1

    fade readout to 0.15

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want the boatyard's numbers worked out?

@math

    erase mathq

    fade plot to 0.12

Revenue is Q times a price of a hundred minus Q, cost is four hundred plus thirty a boat, so profit is seventy Q minus Q squared minus four hundred.
    draw m_box m_line_1

It tops out where its slope, seventy minus two Q, is zero: thirty-five boats.
    draw m_line_2

B: Set profit equal to the floor of seven hundred and twenty-five: two roots, twenty-five and forty-five, and the sales maximiser takes the larger.
    draw m_line_3

    erase m

    fade plot to 1

@after

    erase mathq

    fade readout to 1

    animate f 725 duration 1.2

## Should they maximise profit?
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Friedman and the Roundtable would both hire you.
    good: A sound board member.
    poor: The owners may call a vote.
    none: Satisficing, perhaps.
sources:
    - id: friedman
      title: 'A Friedman Doctrine: The Social Responsibility of Business Is to Increase Its Profits'
      authors: Milton Friedman
      year: 1970
      finding: 'New York Times Magazine, 13 September 1970: a corporate executive spending on social causes spends other people''s money; the one responsibility of business is to increase its profits while staying within the rules of the game (open and free competition without deception or fraud).'
    - id: brt
      title: Statement on the Purpose of a Corporation
      authors: Business Roundtable
      year: 2019
      finding: 'Released 19 August 2019, signed by 181 CEOs: a commitment to deliver value to customers, employees, suppliers, communities and shareholders, replacing statements since 1997 that endorsed shareholder primacy.'
    - id: bt
      title: The Illusory Promise of Stakeholder Governance
      authors: Lucian A. Bebchuk and Roberto Tallarita
      year: 2020
      finding: 'Of 48 signatory companies that answered, 47 said their board of directors had not approved signing the Business Roundtable statement. Cornell Law Review 106: 91–178.'
    - id: hz
      title: Companies Should Maximize Shareholder Welfare Not Market Value
      authors: Oliver Hart and Luigi Zingales
      year: 2017
      finding: 'When shareholders are prosocial and a firm''s harmful activities cannot be separated from its money-making (so owners cannot undo them by giving), the firm should pursue shareholder welfare rather than market value, e.g. by letting shareholders vote. Journal of Law, Finance, and Accounting 2(2): 247–275.'

    text tag_a "a: Friedman" #2f6b8f x 150 y 628 font_size 38 hidden true
    text tag_b "b: stakeholders" #b5482e x 850 y 628 font_size 38 hidden true
    box yard "Boatyard" x 500 y 385 width 250 height 90 radius 10 icon sailboat font_size 36 hidden true
    box ow "Owners" x 500 y 560 width 190 height 90 radius 10 icon "money bag" font_size 36 hidden true
    box wk "Workers" x 190 y 450 width 190 height 90 radius 10 icon hammer font_size 36 hidden true
    box cu "Customers" x 800 y 450 width 220 height 90 radius 10 icon "shopping cart" font_size 36 hidden true
    box su "Suppliers" x 210 y 290 width 190 height 90 radius 10 icon package font_size 36 hidden true
    box tw "Town" x 790 y 290 width 190 height 90 radius 10 icon house font_size 36 hidden true
    portrait friedman cites ["friedman"] x 170 y 470 width 160 of "Milton Friedman" cameo true hidden true
    text essay "“The Social Responsibility of Business\nIs to Increase Its Profits”" #2f6b8f cites ["friedman"] x 720 y 250 font_size 32 hidden true
    text essay_quote "NYT Magazine, 13 Sept 1970" #8f887c x 720 y 175 font_size 28 hidden true
    arrow f_ow yard -> ow #2f6b8f style.stroke_width 6 hidden true
    edge g_wk yard -> wk #8f887c style.stroke_width 1.5 hidden true
    edge g_cu yard -> cu #8f887c style.stroke_width 1.5 hidden true
    edge g_su yard -> su #8f887c style.stroke_width 1.5 hidden true
    edge g_tw yard -> tw #8f887c style.stroke_width 1.5 hidden true
    text law "contracts + law" #8f887c x 500 y 265 font_size 28 hidden true
    arrow r_wk yard -> wk #b5482e style.stroke_width 2.5 hidden true
    arrow r_cu yard -> cu #b5482e style.stroke_width 2.5 hidden true
    arrow r_su yard -> su #b5482e style.stroke_width 2.5 hidden true
    arrow r_tw yard -> tw #b5482e style.stroke_width 2.5 hidden true
    text brt "Business Roundtable 2019:\n181 CEOs" #b5482e cites ["brt"] x 810 y 550 font_size 32 hidden true
    text b4748 "47 of 48 boards:\nnever approved" #2f6b8f cites ["bt"] x 190 y 550 font_size 32 hidden true
    text hz "owners' welfare,\nnot share price" #8a5fa8 cites ["hz"] x 190 y 550 font_size 32 hidden true
    text vote "vote?" #8a5fa8 x 650 y 560 font_size 34 hidden true
    text contested "should maximise?\ncontested" #b5482e x 810 y 545 font_size 40 hidden true
    group hub members ["yard","ow","wk","cu","su","tw","f_ow","g_wk","g_cu","g_su","g_tw","r_wk","r_cu","r_su","r_tw","tag_a","tag_b"] hidden true

    card title "Should they maximise profit?"

So s (@draw yard ow tag_a tag_b@)hould firms maximise profit at all?

Milt (@draw friedman@)on Friedman said yes, in the New York Times Magazine, in September 1970.

His titl (@draw essay@)e says it: the social responsibility of business is to increase its profits,

(@draw essay_quote@) within the rules of the game.

    erase friedman essay essay_quote

A manager w (@draw f_ow@)ho spends on good causes spends other people's money: the owners', the workers', the customers'.

(@draw wk cu su tw g_wk g_cu g_su g_tw law parallel true@) Let people give to causes themselves.

B: In August 2019, a hundred and eighty (@draw brt@)-one chief executives of the (@draw r_wk r_cu r_su r_tw parallel true@) Business Roundtable signed the reply: deliver value to customers, employees, suppliers, communities and shareholders.

    erase law

B: The boatyard owes its welders, its buyers and the town, not just its owners.
    highlight wk cu tw

    erase brt

A manager answerable to five masters can justify anything. And when researchers asked the signe (@draw b4748@)rs' firms, forty-seven of forty-eight said their board never approved signing.

    erase b4748

B: Fair hit. But Oliver Hart and Luigi Zingales, in 2017, offer a road betwee (@draw hz@)n us: maximise the owners' welfare, not just the share price.

B: Owners care about more than money. If the (@highlight tw effect circle@) boatyard can stop fouling the harbour far more cheaply than its owners could clean it up later,

B: ask them, with a vote.
    draw vote

That keeps Friedman's logic, and drops his assumption that pr (@fade f_ow to 0.4@)ofit and harm can always be pulled apart.

    erase vote

What fi (@draw contested@)rms should maximise: contested. That one is values, not evidence.

    pause 0.5

    erase hz contested

    hide hub

    cards fs bins ["Fine: it pays the owners","Not the firm's job"] check each
        * "Higher pay to keep skilled welders" bin "Fine: it pays the owners"
        * "Sponsoring the town team, for publicity" bin "Fine: it pays the owners"
        Not the firm's job: Giving profits to the lifeboat charity
        * "Cutting pollution below the legal limit, at a cost" bin "Not the firm's job"

    ask "Would Friedman approve of each use of the boatyard's money? Sort every card." on fs store f voice b
        intro: Three new cases. First, think like Friedman.
        right: All of them: what raises profit is fine; what only does good is the owners' own choice.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total}. Here, pay and publicity yes; charity and extra cleaning no.

    erase fs

    show hub

    quiz "A supermarket manager is paid by market share and must hit a profit floor. Compared with a profit-maximising rival, she…"
        right: More, at lower prices: she trades profit for sales until the floor stops her.
        wrong: Sales grow by cutting the price until the floor bites: here, more at lower prices.
        + sells more, at lower prices
        * sells less, at higher prices
        * behaves the same

    quiz "A fishing company's owners would give up some profit to stop it wrecking a reef, and could not undo the damage by donating. What would Hart and Zingales advise?" voice b
        right: A vote: the harm can't be undone outside the firm, so the owners' wider welfare should steer it.
        wrong: When the harm can't be undone outside the firm, the owners' own wishes count: here, a vote.
        * Maximise profit; owners can donate
        + Put it to the owners in a vote
        * Let the manager decide

    fade f_ow to 1

B: So what do firms really maximise? Profit where owners can  (@highlight ow yard@)watch and bidders can strike, comfort where they can't, and what they should is still a fight about values.

And if owners can't watch, they can pay the manager to ca (@highlight f_ow effect pulse@)re about profit. Whether that works is the next question.

    pause 0.5

## Where next
